  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nThe thunderstorm threat is still very low confidence for late this \nafternoon into tonight. Quite a bit of cloud cover has lingered into \nthe early afternoon which is slowing heating. And, as opposed to \nyesterday, the mid to upper level wave today is a bit weaker and may \nnot help to organize activity as efficiently, so will need to find a \nway to break the cap. For now these chances look best south of I-90 \ninto northwest IA where the deeper moisture resides. South central \nSD shows the weakest cap late afternoon while parts of northeast NE, \nfar southeast SD and northwest IA see a moderately increasing LLJ \nduring the evening hours, which could focus some thunderstorm \ndevelopment.\n\nWinds turn more northerly tonight into Monday which should allow dew \npoints to settle into the 60s and lower 70s. This will likely keep \nheat indices in the 90s and thus heat headlines will not be \nnecessary. One exception may be across parts of northeast NE and \nnorthwest IA where heat indices should creep above 100 again with \nthe higher dew points. Will have a heat advisory for these locations.\n\nMonday and Monday night should be quiet as more stable air settles \nin. One exception may be later Monday night in central SD as some \nupper level support spreads from western SD into that area. Just \nsome small chances west of the James for now.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday will be on the northern edge of the upper \nlevel ridging which will keep some westerly flow aloft in place. \nThis should allow for a couple of weaker waves to move through. \nWhile precipitation is unlikely, some occasional cloud cover and \nslightly suppressed temperatures will likely keep the more extreme \nheat and humidity at bay, so heat headlines for both of these days \nlooks unlikely.\n\nA stronger wave moves into the area Thursday and should be the next \nbetter chance for showers and thunderstorms. Should see some \nisolated severe storms with this activity. \n\nTemperatures settle back to near normal Friday into next weekend as \nnorthwest flow aloft should bring much more comfortable air into the \nregion with little chance for showers and thunderstorms.",
